<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lakandula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1575">1575</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lakandula was the ruler of Tondo, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> Bay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, when the Spanish arrived in 1570–1571. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> took Manila he accepted Spanish rule and was baptised. He supported the Spanish against the Chinese corsair Limahong in 1574 and died the following year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lakandula was a title rather than a personal name: lakan meant a paramount ruler or paramount datu of one of the large coastal settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> people. The royal notary Hernando Riquel recorded that the lord of Tondo introduced himself as Sibunao Lacandola, which historians read as Bunao, Lakandula, since Spanish writers often took the Tagalog article si for part of a name. Little is known of his early life. The writer Nick Joaquin presumed him to be of native birth, of mixed Tagalog and Kapampangan ancestry.</p><p>Tondo was then the chief port of entry on Manila Bay. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-scott">William Henry Scott</a>, when ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> arrived, the lakan removed their sails and rudders until duties and anchorage fees were paid, then bought their goods himself and traded them with the peoples upstream, so that others could buy Chinese goods only through him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> reached Manila Bay in May 1571, Lakandula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-matanda">Rajah Matanda</a> boarded his ship on 17 May to discuss terms; it was agreed that the Spaniards would land at Manila rather than at Tondo. On 18 May he, Rajah Matanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a> acknowledged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and declared themselves its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. Lakandula helped build a house and a fort for the Spaniards and gave them fourteen pieces of artillery and twelve jars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. He and his sons were baptised, and he took the name Don Carlos Lacandola after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles I of Spain</a>.</p><p>He refused to join the coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarik-sulayman">Tarik Sulayman</a> of Macabebe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-sulayman">Rajah Sulayman</a> that was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bangkusay-channel">Battle of Bangkusay Channel</a>, and Legazpi released two of his nephews taken among the prisoners. Later in 1571 he went with Martin de Goiti on the expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulacan">Bulacan</a>, serving with two ships provided at his own cost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Scott places his death in 1575. His rule over Tondo passed to his grandnephew Agustin de Legazpi, who rose against the Spaniards in the Revolt of the Lakans of 1587–1588 and was killed. His descendants, spread across the Kapampangan region, later styled themselves the Lakandula of Tondo, and a Gremio de Lakandulas was created in 1758 to guard their privileges. The Order of Lakandula, one of the highest honours of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lakandula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
